120 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 63 0 R
/E (The application simulates individual sensors for each patient, which continuously generate vital sign readings. These readings are processed by an analyzer that compares them against predefined threshold values and classifies the results as Normal, Warning or Critical. Whenever an abnormal reading is detected, the system generates an alert, updates the monitoring dashboard and records the event for future reference.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
121 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 63 0 R
/E (The project is developed using Java and Java Swing. Java Swing is used to create an interactive graphical user interface that allows users to register patients, start and stop monitoring, view real-time readings, track alerts and generate reports. The application also incorporates important Java concepts such as Object-Oriented Programming, multithreading, thread synchronization, data structures and file handling. A key feature of VitalWatch is its ability to monitor multiple patients simultaneously This is achieved through Java's multithreading capabilities, where separate sensor tasks generate readings while an analyzer processes them in the background. Thread-safe mechanisms ensure proper communication between these tasks and safe updates to the user interface.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
122 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 63 0 R
/E (Overall, VitalWatch serves as an educational demonstration of a hospital monitoring system, combining graphical user interface development and data management. Although the patient readings are simulated rather than collected from actual medical devices, the project demonstrates the fundamental software concepts involved in building a real-time monitoring application.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
